          hasPlaceholders: false,
        },
        rules: {
          objective: 'Form a better four-card poker hand than the dealer.',
          gameplay: "1. Players place an Ante bet.\n2. Cards are dealt. Players examine their 5-card hand and choose the best 4-card combination.\n3. Making a Choice: Players must either FOLD (losing the Ante) or make a PLAY bet.\n4. Play Bet: Unlike other games, you can bet 1x, 2x, or 3x your Ante.\n5. The Showdown: The Dealer reveals their 6 cards and makes their best 4-card hand. There is no 'dealer qualify' rule.",
          keyRules: [
            "Automatic Bonus: If the player has a Three-of-a-Kind or better, they win a bonus on the Ante bet, regardless of the dealer's hand.",
            'Aces Up: This side bet pays if the player has at least a Pair of Aces in their best four-card hand.',
          ],
          hasPlaceholders: false,
        },
        strategy: {
          basic: 'Strategy is simpler than it looks. You should play (bet 1x) if you have any pair of 10s or better. If you have less than 10s, you should fold.',
          intermediate: 'The 3x Bet: Whenever you have a Pair of Aces or better (including any Three-of-a-Kind), you should always bet the maximum (3x). The statistical advantage on these hands is too large to ignore.',
          advanced: "Dealer Upcard Analysis: If the dealer's visible face-up card is higher than your best card, and you only have a low pair (e.g. 2s or 3s), consider folding even if you 'should' play by the 10s rule, as the dealer has 5 other chances to beat you.",
          tips: [
            'Never bet 2x. Statistically, you should either bet 1x (to see the hand) or 3x (on a winner). The middle ground is mathematically inferior.',
            "Don't chase the Aces Up bet unless you are prepared for high volatility; the house edge is significantly higher than the standard game.",
          ],
          hasPlaceholders: false,
        },
